            <fr:title text="NVIDIA architecture targets">NVIDIA architecture targets</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter><html:p>An <html:code>sm_XX</html:code> target is the compute capability a <fr:link href="/82U4/" title="GPU architecture" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/82U4/" display-uri="82U4" type="local">GPU</fr:link> kernel is compiled against — <html:code>nvcc -arch=sm_90a</html:code> — and it names an instruction surface, not a performance tier. What follows is the coverage a <fr:link href="/EGSP/" title="Tensor Core" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/EGSP/" display-uri="EGSP" type="local">tensor-core</fr:link> kernel actually has to care about: which MMA instruction family exists, and which of the asynchronous data-movement features it depends on came with it.</html:p>
  <html:div style="
    overflow-x: auto;
    max-width: 100%;
  ">
    <html:table>

  <html:tr>
  
  <html:th>Target</html:th>

  
  <html:th>SKU</html:th>

  
  <html:th>Tensor-core path and notable features</html:th>

</html:tr>


  <html:tr>
  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;"><html:code>sm_80</html:code></html:td>

  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;">A100</html:td>

  
  <html:td><html:code>mma.sync</html:code>, <html:code>ldmatrix</html:code>, <html:code>cp.async</html:code>; no <fr:link href="/JHA9/" title="Tensor Memory Accelerator (TMA)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/JHA9/" display-uri="JHA9" type="local">TMA</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/695N/" title="Thread block cluster" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/695N/" display-uri="695N" type="local">clusters</fr:link>, or <fr:link href="/YCAO/" title="Tensor Memory (TMEM)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/YCAO/" display-uri="YCAO" type="local">TMEM</fr:link></html:td>

</html:tr>


  <html:tr>
  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;"><html:code>sm_89</html:code></html:td>

  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;">L40S / RTX 6000 Ada</html:td>

  
  <html:td><html:code>mma.sync</html:code>, <html:code>ldmatrix</html:code>, <html:code>cp.async</html:code>; <fr:link href="/DEQV/" title="Low Precision Data Types" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/DEQV/" display-uri="DEQV" type="local">FP8</fr:link> tensor cores, no TMA or TMEM</html:td>

</html:tr>


  <html:tr>
  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;"><html:code>sm_90a</html:code></html:td>

  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;">H100 / H200</html:td>

  
  <html:td><html:code>wgmma</html:code> + <html:code>mma.sync</html:code>; <fr:link href="/JHA9/" title="Tensor Memory Accelerator (TMA)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/JHA9/" display-uri="JHA9" type="local">TMA</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/695N/" title="Thread block cluster" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/695N/" display-uri="695N" type="local">thread-block clusters</fr:link>, async barriers, <fr:link href="/OGCS8/" title="Distributed Shared Memory (GPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCS8/" display-uri="OGCS8" type="local">DSM</fr:link></html:td>

</html:tr>


  <html:tr>
  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;"><html:code>sm_100a</html:code></html:td>

  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;">B200</html:td>

  
  <html:td><html:code>tcgen05.mma</html:code> + <fr:link href="/YCAO/" title="Tensor Memory (TMEM)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/YCAO/" display-uri="YCAO" type="local">TMEM</fr:link>; 2-CTA MMA (<html:code>cta_group::2</html:code>), <html:code>tcgen05.ld</html:code> / <html:code>.cp</html:code> / <html:code>.shift</html:code></html:td>

</html:tr>


  <html:tr>
  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;"><html:code>sm_103a</html:code></html:td>

  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;">B300</html:td>

  
  <html:td>adds <html:code>tcgen05.ld.red</html:code>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K = 96]]></fr:tex> block-scaled MMA</html:td>

</html:tr>


  <html:tr>
  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;"><html:code>sm_120a</html:code></html:td>

  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;">RTX 5090 / RTX PRO 6000</html:td>

  
  <html:td><html:code>mma.sync</html:code> + <html:code>ldmatrix</html:code>, <fr:link href="/JHA9/" title="Tensor Memory Accelerator (TMA)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/JHA9/" display-uri="JHA9" type="local">TMA</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/695N/" title="Thread block cluster" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/695N/" display-uri="695N" type="local">clusters</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/OGCS8/" title="Distributed Shared Memory (GPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCS8/" display-uri="OGCS8" type="local">DSM</fr:link>; no <html:code>tcgen05</html:code> or TMEM</html:td>

</html:tr>


  <html:tr>
  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;"><html:code>sm_121a</html:code></html:td>

  
  <html:td style="white-space: nowrap;">DGX Spark (GB10)</html:td>

  
  <html:td>same instruction surface as <html:code>sm_120a</html:code>, separate cubin, different SFU rates</html:td>

</html:tr>

</html:table>
  </html:div>
<html:p>Read down the third column and the datacenter line tells one story: each generation moves the matrix multiply further away from the individual thread. <html:code>mma.sync</html:code> is issued by a <fr:link href="/X9VZ/" title="Warp" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/X9VZ/" display-uri="X9VZ" type="local">warp</fr:link> and the operands live in its registers; <html:code>wgmma</html:code> is issued by a warpgroup of four and can read the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> operand straight out of <fr:link href="/8GZN/" title="Shared memory (GPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/8GZN/" display-uri="8GZN" type="local">shared memory</fr:link>; <html:code>tcgen05.mma</html:code> is issued by a single thread on behalf of a <fr:link href="/W2D5/" title="Cooperative Thread Array (CTA)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/W2D5/" display-uri="W2D5" type="local">CTA</fr:link> pair and keeps its accumulator in <fr:link href="/YCAO/" title="Tensor Memory (TMEM)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/YCAO/" display-uri="YCAO" type="local">Tensor Memory (TMEM)</fr:link>, a memory the register file does not see. The data movement follows the same arc — <html:code>cp.async</html:code> is a per-thread copy, <fr:link href="/JHA9/" title="Tensor Memory Accelerator (TMA)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/JHA9/" display-uri="JHA9" type="local">Tensor Memory Accelerator (TMA)</fr:link> is a descriptor a single thread hands to a hardware engine.</html:p><html:p>The trap in the table is <html:code>sm_120a</html:code>. Consumer Blackwell shares the marketing generation with B200 but not the tensor-core path: it has Hopper-era conveniences (TMA, clusters, <fr:link href="/OGCS8/" title="Distributed Shared Memory (GPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCS8/" display-uri="OGCS8" type="local">Distributed Shared Memory (GPU)</fr:link>) and Ampere-era MMA, with no <html:code>tcgen05</html:code> and no TMEM at all. A kernel written against <html:code>sm_100a</html:code> does not merely run slower on a 5090 — it does not compile. Treat <html:code>sm_120a</html:code> as the branch point in a kernel's dispatch, not as a small delta from <html:code>sm_100a</html:code>.</html:p>
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(219, 216, 215);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(109, 103, 101);">Remark.</html:span> The trailing <html:code>a</html:code> means <html:em>architecture-specific</html:em>: the target enables instructions exclusive to that exact chip and gives up forward compatibility, so the resulting cubin will not PTX-JIT onto a later architecture the way a plain <html:code>sm_90</html:code> binary would. Every generation from Hopper on ships its tensor-core ISA only behind an <html:code>a</html:code> target, which is why a real build matrix has one entry per row above rather than one per generation. <html:code>sm_121a</html:code> is the extreme case — the same instruction surface as <html:code>sm_120a</html:code> and still its own cubin.</html:div>
</fr:mainmatter>
